<p>To sell a house by owner in Texas, you price the home, market it yourself or through a flat-fee MLS listing, negotiate with buyers using the standard TREC contract, give the buyer the seller's disclosure notice, and close through a Texas title company. It is legal and very doable. The trade-off is that you take on the pricing, paperwork, showings and negotiation an agent would normally handle.</p>
<p>Here is the full FSBO process, step by step, with the 2026 rules on buyer-agent fees and the forms you will need.</p>

<h2>Step 1: Price the house using real market data</h2>
<p>Pricing is where most FSBO sellers go wrong. Texas is a non-disclosure state, so sale prices are generally not public record. That makes pricing harder than in many states.</p>
<p>Ways to build a realistic price:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Study recent sales and active listings</strong> of similar homes in your subdivision, focusing on size, age, condition and lot.</li>
<li><strong>Do not rely on the appraisal district value.</strong> Your HCAD or other appraisal district value is set for property taxes and can lag or differ from what buyers will pay.</li>
<li><strong>Consider a pre-listing appraisal</strong> from a licensed appraiser. It gives you an independent number to defend in negotiations.</li>
<li><strong>Measure accurately.</strong> Square footage drives buyer comparisons, so measure carefully and state your source.</li>
</ul>
[[product:laser-measure]]
<p>Overpricing is the classic FSBO mistake. A house that sits gets stale, and buyers assume something is wrong with it.</p>

<h2>Step 2: Prepare your disclosures before you list</h2>
<p>Texas Property Code Section 5.008 requires most sellers of single-family homes to give the buyer a written seller's disclosure notice. TREC publishes a Seller's Disclosure Notice form, currently form 55-1, that tracks the statute. It covers known defects, past repairs, flooding, insurance claims, drainage, structural issues and much more.</p>
<p>Fill it out before you list so you can hand it to every serious buyer. Under the TREC resale contract, if a buyer receives the notice after signing, the buyer can terminate within seven days of receiving it. For homes built before 1978, federal law also requires a lead-based paint disclosure. Our guide to the <a href="/blog/texas-seller-disclosure-notice/">Texas seller disclosure notice</a> walks through it section by section.</p>
<p>Gather your documents at the same time: survey, deed, HOA documents, warranties, repair receipts, utility history and your mortgage payoff information.</p>

<h2>Step 3: Market the house (and consider flat-fee MLS)</h2>
<p>Most buyers search online, and most listing websites pull from the MLS. In the Houston area, the MLS is run by the Houston Association of REALTORS (HAR). You cannot post on it directly as an owner.</p>
<p>A flat-fee MLS service gets around that. You pay a licensed broker who is an MLS member to enter and maintain your listing, while you handle pricing, showings and negotiations. You still sign a listing agreement with that broker, so read exactly what is included, what costs extra, and whether any fee is due at closing.</p>
<p>Other marketing basics:</p>
<ul>
<li>Professional photos. Listings with poor photos get skipped.</li>
<li>A clear yard sign with a phone number you will actually answer.</li>
<li>A one-page flyer with price, features, square footage and disclosure availability.</li>
<li>Open houses only if you can staff them safely.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Step 4: Understand buyer-agent compensation in 2026</h2>
<p>Most buyers still work with an agent, so you need a plan for that agent's fee. The rules changed twice recently.</p>
<h3>The 2024 national settlement</h3>
<p>Under practice changes from the National Association of REALTORS settlement that took effect August 17, 2024, offers of compensation to buyer agents can no longer be posted in the MLS. Agents working with buyers must also have a written agreement with the buyer before touring a home. Sellers can still choose to contribute toward a buyer's agent fee; the offer just cannot appear in the MLS.</p>
<h3>Texas written buyer agreements, effective January 1, 2026</h3>
<p>Texas went further with Senate Bill 1968. Starting January 1, 2026, a Texas license holder working with a buyer of residential property must have a written agreement with that buyer before showing any property, or before presenting an offer if nothing is shown. The agreement must state conspicuously that broker compensation is not set by law and is fully negotiable. A showing-only agreement without representation cannot be exclusive and cannot last more than 14 days.</p>
<h3>What it means for you</h3>
<p>The buyer's agent's fee is set in the buyer's own agreement. Paragraph 12B of the TREC contract lets the parties agree that the seller will pay a stated amount or percentage toward the buyer's broker compensation. A buyer may ask for that in their offer. You can accept, counter or decline. Treat it like any other cost in the deal and compare offers by your net proceeds, not just the price.</p>

<h2>Step 5: Negotiate with the TREC contract</h2>
<p>Most Texas resales use the One to Four Family Residential Contract (Resale), currently TREC form 20-19, mandatory for license holders since July 1, 2026. TREC promulgates these forms for license holders, and anyone can download them free from the <a href="https://www.trec.texas.gov/agency-information/contracts">TREC contracts page</a>. Common addenda include the Third Party Financing Addendum, the HOA addendum and the appraisal termination addendum.</p>
<p>Key terms to understand before you sign:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Earnest money and option fee (Paragraph 5):</strong> The buyer delivers both to the escrow agent, usually the title company, within three days after the effective date.</li>
<li><strong>Option period:</strong> The buyer gets an unrestricted right to terminate within the negotiated number of days, by 5:00 p.m. local time on the last day. If they terminate, you keep the option fee and they get the earnest money back. If the sale closes, the option fee is credited to the price.</li>
<li><strong>Survey (Paragraph 6C):</strong> You may be asked to provide your existing survey with a T-47 affidavit or T-47.1 declaration from the Texas Department of Insurance.</li>
<li><strong>Property condition (Paragraph 7):</strong> The buyer can accept the house as-is or as-is with specific repairs. Either way, they can still inspect and negotiate during the option period.</li>
<li><strong>Financing:</strong> A Third Party Financing Addendum gives the buyer a financing contingency. Ask for a pre-approval letter with every financed offer.</li>
</ul>
<p>Because you do not have an agent filling in blanks, hire a Texas real estate attorney to review the contract and any amendments before you sign. It is a small cost compared with a drafting mistake.</p>

<h2>Step 6: Showings and safety</h2>
<p>Inviting strangers into your home is part of selling FSBO, so set rules and stick to them:</p>
<ul>
<li>Schedule showings by appointment only, and get the visitor's full name and phone number first.</li>
<li>Ask unrepresented buyers for a pre-approval letter or proof of funds before a private showing.</li>
<li>Never show the house alone. Have a friend or family member present.</li>
<li>Lock away valuables, prescription drugs, mail with personal information, and firearms.</li>
<li>Use a video doorbell to see who arrives and keep a record of visitors.</li>
</ul>
[[product:ring-video-doorbell]]

<h2>Step 7: Close through a Texas title company</h2>
<p>In Texas, a title company typically serves as escrow agent and closing agent. It holds the earnest money, examines title, issues the title policy, prepares or coordinates the closing documents, collects signatures, records the deed and disburses funds.</p>
<p>A few Texas specifics worth knowing:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Title insurance rates are set by the state.</strong> The Texas Department of Insurance promulgates title premium rates, so the premium for the same policy is the same at every title company. Current <a href="https://tdi.texas.gov/title/documents/titlerates2026.pdf">TDI title rates</a> took effect March 1, 2026. Escrow and other fees are not set by TDI and can vary.</li>
<li><strong>Who pays the owner's policy is negotiable.</strong> Paragraph 6A of the contract has a box for seller or buyer.</li>
<li><strong>Escrow fees are split by default.</strong> The TREC contract assigns one-half of the escrow fee to each party unless you agree otherwise.</li>
<li><strong>No state transfer tax.</strong> Texas does not charge a real estate transfer tax.</li>
</ul>
<p>Estimate your full closing bill with the <a href="/tools/closing-cost-calculator/">closing cost calculator</a> and our guide to <a href="/blog/closing-costs-in-texas/">closing costs in Texas</a>.</p>

<h2>FSBO vs. listing with an agent: the cost comparison</h2>
<p>Commissions are negotiable, and every sale is different. The table uses hypothetical round numbers purely to show how to compare. Swap in your real quotes.</p>
<table>
